12568	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s7","entry":{"id":"review-d27349e274a826fe80375ec0b81448c2","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:57:59.861Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The foundation-only scope implements the recorded s7 split without moving s9/s10 call sites.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md explicitly says 's7 is read as the foundations only' and lists hooks, destructive denial, cancellation, typed tools, tools.call and the report-mode scan as its five effective criteria. It assigns site moves and wrapper deletion to s9/s10. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Retain this scope and the explicit handover.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The existing callTool implementation can be consumed unchanged without addressing upstream prerequisites.","evidence":"The plan records: 'before s7: callTool must check that the registered tool under tool.id is the definition passed (same object or same outputSchema)'. Actual callTool calls executeTool(tool.id, ...) and casts result.data to Static<T['outputSchema']> without comparing definitions. registerTool permits overwriting an id. Consequently a different registered schema can validate data that is then returned under the supplied definition's incompatible type. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts]","action":"Resolve the recorded prerequisite before relying on typed calls: compare the registered definition or schema identity, accommodating withFailureNet copies, and reject mismatches. Preserve the public signature.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The current executor and shell helper lack the cancellation checks this Story proposes to add.","evidence":"executeTool passes deps.signal to gate().ask but has no signal check before tool.execute. runShell spawns before installing its abort listener and never checks signal.aborted. runShellBytes explicitly returns an aborted result before spawning. The inspected refresh tools use the fixed limits described; git_cat_file already forwards its signal with timeoutMs: 30_000. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch-refs.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/rev-parse.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/symbolic-ref.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/ls-tree.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/cat-file.ts]","action":"Retain these receiving-side changes and distinguish them from s9's signal creation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"ghFailureOf currently sets failure.original only from probe text.","evidence":"Its spawn-error branch returns original: o.stderr.trim(). Only the subsequent non-zero-exit classification derives original solely from probe output. The design repeats the broader claim in its migration description and invariant. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts]","action":"Qualify the statement to ordinary command-exit failures and preserve the existing spawn-error original field. Include that branch in diagnostic compatibility tests.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"defineGhFamily and the approval/stream infrastructure provide the stated reusable foundations.","evidence":"defineGhFamily builds schemas, registers approval/limit metadata and returns a wrapped family without registerTool. Its custom output builder supports tracker-specific shaping. StreamHandler receives params, send, signal and optional trusted context. ApprovalBroker broadcasts to subscribed streams, denies no-client, and cancels pending requests on abort. The existing project item-add tool returns success with {number, url}; ghAddProjectItem only needs success. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/project.ts, src/workflow/tracker/github.ts, src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/approval.ts, src/daemon/approval-broker.ts]","action":"Reuse these seams. Preserve the family output's literal op discriminator when specifying tracker schemas.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The concrete git-definition typing inventory covers the tools required by the named cutover callers.","evidence":"Both grounding.ts runDiff and changed-files.ts collectDiff call gitDiffTool.execute directly. diff.ts declares 'export const gitDiffTool: Tool', which widens its schemas and leaves outputSchema optional. The proposed definition-change inventory lists seven refresh tools but omits diff.ts, so these named consumers cannot use callTool(gitDiffTool, ...) under its unchanged TypedTool constraint. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts, src/workflow/runners/build/changed-files.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts]","action":"Include gitDiffTool and audit the remaining s9/s10 tool imports for the same widening. Extend positive and negative compile-time fixtures to the complete consumer inventory.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"tools.call can use the existing generic stream transport and trusted connection metadata without changing their shapes.","evidence":"directDispatch combines request and connection abort signals and invokes streamHandler(params, send, signal, context). Server delivery overwrites frame ids with the wire request id. TrustedRpcContext has connectionIdentity and optional connectionExecution, with no sessionId. IpcClient already offers an optional generic stream method and a close handle that destroys its socket. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/request-context.ts, src/shared/ipc-client.ts, src/daemon/threads/method-class.ts]","action":"Keep the proposed handler and method-class additions; document that the internal ToolDeps request counter is distinct from wire correlation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The receiving-side additions require no persisted-data migration and can preserve existing callers.","evidence":"ShellOptions already accepts an optional signal; ShellResult can gain an optional aborted field. readGit currently takes four arguments, so an optional fifth parameter preserves callers. git_cat_file's input schema currently lacks timeoutMs and uses a fixed 30_000 limit; making that field optional preserves existing valid inputs. OpDef and the registry describe runtime tools rather than stored records. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/cat-file.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts]","action":"Keep additions optional and retain regression tests for calls without signals, default cat-file limits and non-opt-in gh failures.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The Story respects the recorded ownership boundaries and adequately documents its new interaction relationships.","evidence":"The plan explicitly assigns the four git commands and narrow tracker family to s7, exempts indexer/self-update, and defers production hooks and site moves. The design's tools.call prose specifies one owning stream per call, daemon-wide approval subscribers, separate answer RPCs, trusted metadata direction and additive compatibility, consistent with server.ts and ApprovalBroker. No persisted entity relationship or UI layout is introduced, and no companion file is linked for rendering verification. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/request-context.ts, src/daemon/approval-broker.ts]","action":"Retain the ownership prose; a sequence diagram is optional rather than a blocking requirement for these adequately specified interactions.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The proposed runShell changes guarantee that a running git command stops at the shared deadline.","evidence":"runShell's abort callback is only 'child.kill(\\'SIGKILL\\')'; it creates no process group and resolves on child 'close'. A git transport or helper descendant can survive and keep stdout/stderr pipes open after the parent dies. The same file's runShellBytes explicitly uses detached process groups and negative-pid killing 'so a kill reaches the grandchildren that hold the output pipes open'. Adding only a pre-spawn check and forwarding the signal preserves this deadline-breaking path. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Specify process-tree termination and cleanup for runShell, or reuse equivalent byte-helper lifecycle handling. Test a git stand-in that starts a descendant holding output pipes, and require bounded settlement after abort.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Unsafe git_update_ref input is rejected without requesting approval under the specified validation mechanism.","evidence":"executeTool performs schema validation, then builds and asks for approval, then calls tool.execute. assertSafeArg rejects NUL inside execute via withFailureNet. A value such as 'refs/heads/x\\u0000' satisfies the proposed starts-with-refs/ pattern but reaches the destructive approval gate before assertSafeArg runs. With no subscriber it returns denied/no-client instead of the promised rejected input failure. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts]","action":"Express every safety condition needed before approval in the new tool's input schema, including NUL exclusion, or specify an approved pre-approval validation seam. Test unsafe input with both absent and attached approval subscribers.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The five effective acceptance criteria have explicit proposed test mappings using existing test seams.","evidence":"The effective criteria in tool-registry.md map to the design's workflow-hook/approval tests, abort/shared-deadline tests, typed tool and tracker tests, and tools.call/scan tests. executor.ts exposes _setApprovalGateForTests, shell-helper.ts exposes timeout scaling, and callTool's generic schema signature supports the proposed @ts-expect-error fixtures. tsconfig.json includes src/**/*.ts while excluding __tests__ and *.test.ts, confirming the need for a separate type-test configuration and root exclusion. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, tsconfig.json, package.json]","action":"Keep these mappings and add regression cases for the registry-definition mismatch, omitted git_diff typing, descendant-process cancellation and pre-approval unsafe-input findings above. This assesses the proposed tests, not execution of tests that have yet to be implemented.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":4,"med":1,"low":7,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-26T14:57:59.861Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
