9186	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s7","entry":{"id":"review-e1509f8538271f2850a5a1b086471914","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:54:47.279Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The foundations-only scope covers the five effective s7 acceptance criteria.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md, “s7 split into s7, s9 and s10”, explicitly assigns hooks, destructive-call denial, cancellation foundations, typed tools/tracker family, tools.call and report-mode scanning to s7. The design addresses each and leaves caller migration to s9/s10. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Retain this explicit scope and acceptance mapping.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The current executor validates input before approval, returns tool-authored failures unchanged, and lacks cancellation checks before execution.","evidence":"executeTool validates input before resolveApprovalClass/buildApprovalRequest, passes deps.signal to gate().ask, then directly invokes tool.execute. Its result handling contains “if (!result.success) return result;”. ORIGINAL_MAX_CHARS is 4000 and is applied by failed(). [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Preserve this pipeline while adding cancellation checks.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The two shell helpers currently have the different cancellation and process-group behavior described.","evidence":"runShell spawns without detached, aborts using child.kill('SIGKILL'), and resolves on error/close without an entry abort check. runShellBytes checks options.signal?.aborted, spawns detached on non-win32, and kills using process.kill(-child.pid, 'SIGKILL'); its kill paths do not schedule bounded settlement. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Proceed with the shared kill-and-settle helper and the proposed descendant tests.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The proposed provenance comparison keeps exported gh tools, including gh_tracker, callable through callTool.","evidence":"defineGhFamily returns “withGhFailureNet(tool as unknown as GhFamilyTool)”. Existing exports such as ghProjectListTool also directly export withGhFailureNet({...}), and registerGhTools registers those exported wrapped objects. With the proposed marking, definitionOf(exportedWrapper) is the inner object. Consequently “(definitionOf(registered) ?? registered) !== tool” is true when callTool receives the exported wrapper, rejecting legitimate gh calls, including the newly exported family. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/project.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts]","action":"Define a consistent public definition identity for gh exports and registration, then revise the provenance comparison/wrapping arrangement accordingly. Cover real exported legacy tools and defineGhFamily exports, not only synthetic unwrapped definitions.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"A tool omitted from gitToolDefinitions necessarily produces a compile error at its declaration.","evidence":"The current git/index.ts individually imports and registers definitions. Replacing that inventory with “as const satisfies readonly TypedTool[]” checks the listed elements; a separate declaration using satisfies TypedTool does not establish membership in that list. An omitted definition can still compile. The design's later file-versus-list runtime inventory test can detect the omission, but the claimed compile-time guarantee cannot. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts]","action":"Remove the compile-time completeness claim and make the independent file/export inventory comparison mandatory, or specify a generated inventory mechanism that actually enforces completeness.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The reused op-table supports the stated additive field-pattern and argv extensions without already providing those capabilities.","evidence":"FieldSpec currently contains kind, values, minimum, description and empty; fieldSchema's string branch has no pattern. Arg contains pos, spread, last, flag and lit. buildArgv implements those variants only. buildInputSchema compares shared specs using JSON.stringify with description and values removed. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts]","action":"Keep pattern as a string and retain regression tests for existing argv and shared-field comparisons.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The proposed FieldSpec pattern and internal argv variants can be additive without rewriting stored records.","evidence":"FieldSpec and Arg are code-defined operation descriptions consumed by fieldSchema/buildArgv. Existing fieldSchema behavior can be preserved when pattern is absent, and existing Arg variants remain unchanged. These structures are not persisted by the inspected op-table implementation. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts]","action":"Retain the optional field and existing-operation regression tests; no storage migration is needed for these additions.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Building the tracker family and four git commands in s7, while deferring caller moves, follows the recorded scope decisions.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md records the user's decisions: “s7 builds the narrow typed tracker family” and “s7 builds config, ls-remote, update-ref and rm”. Its split section moves site migration and old-path deletion to s9/s10. The design follows those boundaries. Its prose also identifies stream ownership and wrapper-to-definition direction/cardinality sufficiently; no ERD is necessary for these transient relationships. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts]","action":"Retain the plan reference and relationship prose. No linked generated visual was supplied or claimed verified.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The diagnostic seam correctly accounts for existing gh failure classification and executor failure passthrough.","evidence":"ghFailureOf returns spawn-error original from o.stderr.trim(); ordinary command-exit branches derive original only from probe output. defineGhFamily forwards the failure before shape(), and executeTool returns failed tool results unchanged. Thus the proposed opt-in seam must apply its own 4000-character cap as specified. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Implement the cap at the seam and preserve existing failure objects with the proposed regression fixtures.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every effective s7 acceptance criterion has a concrete proposed test mapping.","evidence":"The plan defines five effective criteria. Section 6 maps each to named tests: workflow hooks, approval denial, cancellation/deadline behavior, typed definitions/new tools/tracker operations, and tools.call/scanning. The existing executor exposes _setApprovalGateForTests, and tsconfig.json excludes __tests__ and *.test.ts, supporting the proposed runtime seams and separate typecheck configuration. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, tsconfig.json, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts]","action":"Retain these mappings. Extend identity tests to the actual already-wrapped gh exports identified above; test names alone do not establish that those cases pass.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":1,"low":8,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-26T15:54:47.279Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
