11051	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s7","entry":{"id":"review-49dac2d7abe1f31b39c7d83134210403","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:08:53.634Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD covers the five effective foundation criteria and leaves caller migration to s9 and s10.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md states 's7 is read as the foundations only' and lists hooks, destructive approval, cancellation, typed tools, tools.call and the reporting scan. Its following paragraph assigns site moves and deletion to s9/s10. The LLD designs these foundations and preserves those assignments. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Use the plan's recorded effective criteria when assessing implementation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The executor currently checks typed input before approval, passes only deps.signal to the gate, and returns tool failures unchanged.","evidence":"executeTool validates input before buildApprovalRequest, calls 'gate().ask(request, deps.signal)', then calls tool.execute without a cancellation recheck. Its result handling contains 'if (!result.success) return result;'. Hooks follow successful output validation. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, src/daemon/tools/hooks.ts]","action":"Preserve these pipeline and failure-preservation properties while adding cancellation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The existing git failure-net wrapper converts unsafe-argument errors and other throws into rejected failures itself.","evidence":"withFailureNet catches UnsafeArgError and returns rejectInput, but its other-exception branch is explicitly 'throw err;'. executeTool, rather than this wrapper, converts those other exceptions into rejected results. Section 2.19 incorrectly attributes both conversions to the wrapper while promising unchanged behavior. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Correct the wrapper contract to distinguish UnsafeArgError handling from executor-level exception handling; keep the provenance edit behavior-preserving.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The provenance mechanism must accommodate git registration copies and already-wrapped gh exports.","evidence":"registerGitTools calls 'register(withFailureNet(gitStatusTool))' and similarly wraps the other definitions. withFailureNet spreads the original. ghProjectListTool is exported as 'withGhFailureNet({...})', and defineGhFamily returns withGhFailureNet. callTool currently dispatches solely by tool.id and casts result.data; registerTool overwrites existing ids. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/project.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts]","action":"Implement and test the proposed immediate-original chain, including exported gh objects and spread replacements.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Field patterns and composed endpoint/query arguments require additive extensions to the existing gh op table.","evidence":"FieldSpec has no pattern property. fieldSchema currently combines string and expr into Type.String with minLength and description. Arg contains pos, spread, last, flag and lit only. buildInputSchema compares shared specs using JSON.stringify after omitting description and values. Existing custom output builders provide the proposed parsing seam. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts]","action":"Keep existing variants unchanged and include string patterns in shared-field equality checks.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"runToolHooks retains the signature stated in section 2.6.","evidence":"The actual declaration is 'runToolHooks(tool: Tool, input: unknown, result: ToolResult, deps: ToolDeps): Promise<readonly HookFailure[]>'. The LLD declares input: ToolInput and Promise<HookFailure[]> while describing the function as unchanged. The readonly result is part of the existing shared contract. [files: src/daemon/tools/hooks.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Copy the real signature into the LLD and retain its unknown input and readonly failure array.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A tools.call stream can reuse the existing generic stream cancellation path without adding sessionId to trusted context.","evidence":"StreamHandler receives params, send, signal and context. directDispatch combines stream and trusted lifetime signals, passes the combined signal to the handler, and its cancellation callback aborts the controller. TrustedRpcContext exposes connectionIdentity but no sessionId. The server closes connections by aborting their controller. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/request-context.ts, src/daemon/handler-runtime.ts, src/daemon/threads/method-class.ts]","action":"Add the handler and method-class entry as designed; test through actual dispatch, including suppression of frames after cancellation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The gh diagnostic seam can be opt-in while preserving existing command-exit failure objects.","evidence":"ghFailureOf currently sets spawn-error original to trimmed stderr, while command-exit original comes from nonempty probe output. defineGhFamily forwards failures before shaping output. executeTool returns failed tool results unchanged, so the proposed 4000-character cap must indeed be applied by the new seam itself. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/op-table.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Retain the proposed opt-in default and regression tests for existing failure objects; no stored-record migration is needed for this seam.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The four git tools, tracker family and provenance prerequisite belong in narrowed s7 rather than requiring s8 or caller cutover here.","evidence":"The plan explicitly assigns the tracker family and config, ls-remote, update-ref and rm to s7, states that s8 is not added to dependsOn, and records the callTool identity prerequisite. Its split section assigns the actual caller moves to s9/s10. The LLD's ownership descriptions preserve those boundaries. The new runtime relationships are explained in prose: executor-to-context, lifecycle ownership, wrapper-to-original links, and stream-to-call cancellation; no companion file is linked for rendering. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts, src/daemon/server.ts]","action":"Keep production caller moves outside this build. A sequence diagram could improve navigation, but the supplied relationship prose is adequate.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"An interrupted gh auth probe is currently treated as having no probe text.","evidence":"After awaiting the probe, ghExec unconditionally assigns 'outcome.probe = { code: p.code, output: ... }' using captured stdout and stderr. It does not inspect p.aborted or p.timedOut. ghFailureOf subsequently uses that output for classification and failure.original. A killed probe that emitted partial text therefore does not necessarily become 'no probe text', contrary to section 5. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/gh/helpers.ts]","action":"Either document preservation of captured partial probe text, or explicitly design a change that discards interrupted-probe output and test its classification consequences.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shell helpers need the proposed pre-spawn cancellation check and bounded settlement.","evidence":"runShell spawns before installing its abort listener, kills only child, and resolves on error or close. runShellBytes checks an already-aborted signal and uses detached process-group killing, but finish is invoked only from error or close. Neither has a post-kill settlement timer. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Retain tests for already-aborted signals, descendant-held pipes, timeout and stderr overflow when consolidating the lifecycle.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each effective s7 acceptance criterion has a concrete proposed test mapping using existing test seams.","evidence":"The acceptance table maps ac1–ac5 to hook, approval, cancellation, typed-tool and stream/scan tests respectively. The repository provides _setApprovalGateForTests, hook registration/reset, registry reset and shell timeout scaling. tsconfig.json excludes __tests__ and *.test.ts but includes other src TypeScript, supporting the need for a separately included and normally excluded type-fixture suite. This verifies test design, not execution of the proposed tests. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/hooks.ts, src/daemon/tools/registry.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, tsconfig.json, docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Implement the mapped tests, correcting the contract discrepancies above. The analyzer returned an incomplete, documentation-focused bundle, so implementation grounding here used direct file reads.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":3,"low":9,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-26T17:08:53.634Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
